## 0. READ-ME FOR THE EXECUTING AGENT

You are executing a **retrospective conformity survey and technical-file uplift**, not a redesign. Read this section before anything else.

**0.1 What this job is.** Take a mature (~10–15 year) radiation test system with a full but unstructured data pack, and produce a defensible, auditable Technical Construction File (TCF) demonstrating clause-by-clause alignment with IEC 61010-1 — *without physical modification of the hardware* — then extract the method as the compliance-by-design template for the Generation-2 system.

**0.2 What "compliant" means here.** An internally controlled technical assurance position: clause-by-clause evidence mapping, signed off under the organisation's own document control. **Explicitly NOT** third-party certification, CE marking, or UKCA marking. The exact approved wording is in §8. Never exceed it.

**0.3 Source precedence.** The data pack contains four overlapping guidance documents (A, B, C, D) written at different stages of maturity. When they conflict, resolve in this order:

1. **This specification** (which records the reconciliation decisions).
2. **Document A** (`A_-_IEC61010-Compliance-Manual_A.docx`) — the controlling process manual and document architecture. Most recent, formally authored, Rev A.
3. **Document C** (`C_-_Retrospective_IEC_61010_Uplift_Manual.docx`) — the execution detail library: SYS-xxx numbering, gates, checklists, boundary questions, test formats, gap-register mechanics. Use for *how*, subordinate to A for *what*.
4. **Document B** (`B_-_Master_Compliance_Blueprint.docx` + `B__IEC_61010_TCF_Document_Tree.xlsx`) — clause-level technical parameters and provisional acceptance values (0.1 Ω PE bond, 3.0 mm clearance / 6.3 mm creepage, 10° tilt, 150 N push, UL 94 V-0/V-1, environmental profile 5–40 °C / 2000 m / 80 % RH). Treat every clause number and numeric limit in B as **provisional until confirmed against the licensed edition of the standard** (A §3 note, A §9).
5. **Document D** (`D_-_IEC_61010_UPLIFT_MANUAL.docx` + `D_-_svg_TREE.txt`) — the raw working chat log and the 18-branch content tree. Background and content checklists only; its 18 branches are *content sections*, not separate documents.

**0.4 Rule against fabrication.** Never invent measured values, part numbers, ratings, dates, serial numbers, revision letters, test results, or clause text. Where a fact is unknown, insert a structured placeholder (§5.3) and raise a gap-register entry. A TCF with honest gaps is the deliverable; a TCF with plausible-looking invented data is a failure of the entire exercise.

**0.5 Depth rule (from the commissioning instruction).** Deliverables are produced at three depths — this is the central editorial decision of the job:

- **Class F — FULL AUTHOR.** Safety, risk, and cross-reference/compliance documents are written complete and thorough: every clause, every hazard, every requirement, every traceability link, with placeholders only where evidence is physically absent.
- **Class D — DELTA LIST.** Existing engineering design data (schematics, drawings, PCB, FPGA, LabVIEW, BOM) is **not recreated**. The deliverable is a per-document *update register*: what must change, what attribute is missing, what revision action is required. Reference, don't duplicate (A, Rule 2).
- **Class R — REFERENCE / INDEX.** Third-party material (datasheets, certificates, calibration certs, facility rules, the UKAEA context paper) is catalogued in the Master Document List with a role tag and citation, nothing more.

**0.6 Human dependencies.** Some steps cannot be completed from the data pack alone: physical photo survey, bench tests (PE continuity, output short-circuit, power-loss/restore, thermal, stability), gate sign-offs, and residual-risk acceptance. Flag these as `HUMAN-ACTION` items in the gap register; do not simulate their outputs.

**0.7 Expected state of the incoming data pack.** Assume it is substantial but non-uniform: strong on design definition (schematics, drawings, firmware) after 10–15 years of evolution; weak on formal safety artefacts (no boundary statement, no hazard register in IEC 61010 form, no clause matrix, no verification traceability, patchy labelling/manual coverage). §4 defines what the *final* compliant pack looks like so that every found item can be slotted into its home and every absence becomes a recorded gap rather than a silent hole.

---

## 1. MISSION AND SUCCESS DEFINITION

**1.1 Mission.** Retrospectively survey the Gen-1 system against IEC 61010-1 (with screening of IEC 61010-2-030 and awareness of 61010-2-201), uplift the data pack into a controlled TCF, close or formally accept every gap, issue the Safety Document and internal Statement of Compliance, and convert the method and every finding into the Generation-2 compliance-by-design baseline.

**1.2 Target claim (default — confirm at Q1).**
> "The system has been retrospectively reviewed and documented against the applicable principles and requirements of IEC/BS EN 61010-1, with additional consideration of applicable particular standards, including external test/measurement circuits where relevant. This review does not constitute third-party certification unless separately assessed by an accredited body." (C §0)

For Gen-2: "Safety requirements captured at concept stage and designed to meet the applicable IEC 61010 family requirements from the outset." (C §1.2)

**1.3 Definition of done.** The job is complete when ALL of the following hold:

1. Every document in the Canonical Register (§4) exists at its declared class (F authored / D delta-registered / R indexed), under document control with revision status and owner.
2. The Compliance Matrix (SYS-COMP-002) covers **every clause** of the confirmed edition of IEC 61010-1 plus the 2-030 screening decision, each with status ∈ {Compliant by design, Compliant by evidence, Compliant by documented rationale, Partially compliant, Not evidenced, Not applicable} and a justification.
3. The Hazard Register, Single-Fault Analysis, and Safety Requirement Specification are complete (no placeholder rows in the analysis itself — placeholders are permitted only in *evidence* fields).
4. Every gap is in SYS-GAP-001 with type, risk, closure route (bench verification / engineering analysis / residual-risk acceptance — A §8), and status. No gap silently closed by hardware modification; any item that cannot be closed by the three routes is escalated as a significant finding, not accepted quietly.
5. The Safety Document (SYS-SAF-010) is issued to the structure of A §7, readable cover-to-cover, everything else cited by number and revision.
6. Gates 0–5 (C §18) have recorded sign-offs (human).
7. SYS-NG-REQ-001 and the Gen-2 Checklist exist, with every material finding converted to a requirement tagged mandatory / recommended / legacy-carryover, mapped to the Gen-2 design-review gates (A §10: SRR → PDR → CDR → first use).

---

## 2. SYSTEM IDENTITY — GROUND TRUTH ESTABLISHED FROM THE DATA PACK

Treat the following as established facts (source in brackets). Anything not listed here is unconfirmed.

- **System:** heavy-ion radiation test system for image-sensor characterisation; adaptable to other radiation forms via extension of propagation cables. Result of 10–15 years continuous development; mature and stable. [A §1–2; D]
- **Form factor:** 19-inch rackmount trolley, ~1 m high, mobile on casters. [A §2; B §1]
- **Power:** single 230 V AC mains feed; internal derivation of low-voltage DC of the order of 12 V. [A §2]
- **Field side:** signal I/O propagated over cable runs of ~25 m (A §2; note C's boundary sketch says "several metres to 15 m or more depending setup" — treat length as configuration-dependent, record per baseline) across **five signal classes: bias/sense, differential, LVDS, RF coaxial, clock**, to a PCB on a support fixture exposed in the beam line. [A §2, §5 Rule 1]
- **Existing protection architecture (to be mapped as clause evidence, largely already designed):**
  - Split hardened/recoverable protection path for Single Event Latch-up (SEL).
  - Dual-threshold bias fault detection with configurable dwell timers.
  - Verified **monitors-armed interlock, gated before beam-on**.
  - Sub-second FPGA-latched event timestamping supplementing a 1 Hz polling loop. [A §2]
- **Control stack:** FPGA fault-supervision layer + LabVIEW supervisory software (PXI context referenced in C). [A §3; C §11]
- **Regulatory posture:** in-house facility instrument, not placed on the market; CE/UKCA not pursued through this exercise; EMC (IEC 61326 series) and functional safety (IEC 61508) out of scope, the latter noted only where the protection architecture already provides evidence. [A §1, §3]
- **Environment/context:** radiation beamline facility. The UKAEA paper (UKAEA-CCFE-CP(25)12, Maslov et al., radiation effects in optical fibres during JET D-T campaigns, Culham) is **domain context only** — it characterises the class of facility/radiation environment; it is not a compliance input. [file: UKAEACCFECP2512.PDF — note: the file is actually a ZIP of 14 page images + OCR text, not a true PDF]
- **Author/owner of the exercise:** Paul (A title block). System designation and document reference are blank in A — must be assigned (Q6).

